  28:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c **** 
  29:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c **** #include "arm_math.h"
  30:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c **** 
  31:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c **** /**
  32:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * @ingroup groupFilters
  33:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  */
  34:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c **** 
  35:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c **** /**
  36:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * @defgroup LMS_NORM Normalized LMS Filters
  37:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  *
  38:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * This set of functions implements a commonly used adaptive filter.
  39:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * It is related to the Least Mean Square (LMS) adaptive filter and includes an additional normaliz
  40:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * factor which increases the adaptation rate of the filter.
  41:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * The CMSIS DSP Library contains normalized LMS filter functions that operate on Q15, Q31, and flo
  42:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  *
  43:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * A normalized least mean square (NLMS) filter consists of two components as shown below.
  44:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * The first component is a standard transversal or FIR filter.
  45:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * The second component is a coefficient update mechanism.
  46:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * The NLMS filter has two input signals.
  47:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * The "input" feeds the FIR filter while the "reference input" corresponds to the desired output o
  48:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * That is, the FIR filter coefficients are updated so that the output of the FIR filter matches th
  49:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * The filter coefficient update mechanism is based on the difference between the FIR filter output
  50:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * This "error signal" tends towards zero as the filter adapts.
  51:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * The NLMS processing functions accept the input and reference input signals and generate the filt
  52:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * \image html LMS.gif "Internal structure of the NLMS adaptive filter"
  53:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  *
  54:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * The functions operate on blocks of data and each call to the function processes
  55:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * <code>blockSize</code> samples through the filter.
  56:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * <code>pSrc</code> points to input signal, <code>pRef</code> points to reference signal,
  57:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * <code>pOut</code> points to output signal and <code>pErr</code> points to error signal.
  58:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * All arrays contain <code>blockSize</code> values.
  59:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  *
  60:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * The functions operate on a block-by-block basis.
  61:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * Internally, the filter coefficients <code>b[n]</code> are updated on a sample-by-sample basis.
  62:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * The convergence of the LMS filter is slower compared to the normalized LMS algorithm.
  63:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  *
  64:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * \par Algorithm:
  65:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * The output signal <code>y[n]</code> is computed by a standard FIR filter:
  66:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * <pre>
  67:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  *     y[n] = b[0] * x[n] + b[1] * x[n-1] + b[2] * x[n-2] + ...+ b[numTaps-1] * x[n-numTaps+1]
  68:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * </pre>
  69:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  *
  70:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * \par
  71:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * The error signal equals the difference between the reference signal <code>d[n]</code> and the fi
  72:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * <pre>
  73:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  *     e[n] = d[n] - y[n].
  74:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * </pre>
  75:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  *
  76:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * \par
  77:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * After each sample of the error signal is computed the instanteous energy of the filter state var
  78:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * <pre>
  79:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  *    E = x[n]^2 + x[n-1]^2 + ... + x[n-numTaps+1]^2.
  80:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * </pre>
  81:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * The filter coefficients <code>b[k]</code> are then updated on a sample-by-sample basis:
  82:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * <pre>
  83:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  *     b[k] = b[k] + e[n] * (mu/E) * x[n-k],  for k=0, 1, ..., numTaps-1
  84:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * </pre>
ARM GAS  C:\Users\Lamp\AppData\Local\Temp\ccyqEvsa.s 			page 3


  85:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * where <code>mu</code> is the step size and controls the rate of coefficient convergence.
  86:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  *\par
  87:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * In the APIs, <code>pCoeffs</code> points to a coefficient array of size <code>numTaps</code>.
  88:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * Coefficients are stored in time reversed order.
  89:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * \par
  90:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * <pre>
  91:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  *    {b[numTaps-1], b[numTaps-2], b[N-2], ..., b[1], b[0]}
  92:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * </pre>
  93:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * \par
  94:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * <code>pState</code> points to a state array of size <code>numTaps + blockSize - 1</code>.
  95:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * Samples in the state buffer are stored in the order:
  96:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * \par
  97:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * <pre>
  98:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  *    {x[n-numTaps+1], x[n-numTaps], x[n-numTaps-1], x[n-numTaps-2]....x[0], x[1], ..., x[blockSize
  99:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * </pre>
 100:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * \par
 101:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * Note that the length of the state buffer exceeds the length of the coefficient array by <code>bl
 102:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * The increased state buffer length allows circular addressing, which is traditionally used in FIR
 103:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * to be avoided and yields a significant speed improvement.
 104:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * The state variables are updated after each block of data is processed.
 105:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * \par Instance Structure
 106:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * The coefficients and state variables for a filter are stored together in an instance data struct
 107:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * A separate instance structure must be defined for each filter and
 108:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * coefficient and state arrays cannot be shared among instances.
 109:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * There are separate instance structure declarations for each of the 3 supported data types.
 110:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  *
 111:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * \par Initialization Functions
 112:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * There is also an associated initialization function for each data type.
 113:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * The initialization function performs the following operations:
 114:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * - Sets the values of the internal structure fields.
 115:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * - Zeros out the values in the state buffer.
 116:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * To do this manually without calling the init function, assign the follow subfields of the instan
 117:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * numTaps, pCoeffs, mu, energy, x0, pState. Also set all of the values in pState to zero.
 118:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * For Q7, Q15, and Q31 the following fields must also be initialized;
 119:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * recipTable, postShift
 120:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  *
 121:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * \par
 122:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * Instance structure cannot be placed into a const data section and it is recommended to use the i
 123:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * \par Fixed-Point Behavior:
 124:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * Care must be taken when using the Q15 and Q31 versions of the normalised LMS filter.
 125:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * The following issues must be considered:
 126:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * - Scaling of coefficients
 127:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * - Overflow and saturation
 128:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  *
 129:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * \par Scaling of Coefficients:
 130:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * Filter coefficients are represented as fractional values and
 131:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * coefficients are restricted to lie in the range <code>[-1 +1)</code>.
 132:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * The fixed-point functions have an additional scaling parameter <code>postShift</code>.
 133:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * At the output of the filter's accumulator is a shift register which shifts the result by <code>p
 134:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * This essentially scales the filter coefficients by <code>2^postShift</code> and
 135:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * allows the filter coefficients to exceed the range <code>[+1 -1)</code>.
 136:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * The value of <code>postShift</code> is set by the user based on the expected gain through the sy
 137:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  *
 138:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * \par Overflow and Saturation:
 139:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * Overflow and saturation behavior of the fixed-point Q15 and Q31 versions are
 140:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  * described separately as part of the function specific documentation below.
 141:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_lms_norm_f32.c ****  */
